        {
            "number": 3,
            "revision": "895078a796e18a9de07bcb3440fcc3087d25c565",
            "parents": [
                "1c48a8321d91e1eaccda4bfd4dbfb896557edf1d"
            ],
            "ref": "refs/changes/17/84517/3",
            "uploader": {
                "name": "Eric Yang",
                "email": "eric.yang.wiwynn@gmail.com",
                "username": "fornchu110"
            },
            "createdOn": 1760698039,
            "author": {
                "name": "Eric Yang",
                "email": "eric.yang.wiwynn@gmail.com",
                "username": "fornchu110"
            },
            "kind": "NO_CODE_CHANGE",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 18,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Andrew Geissler",
                        "email": "geissonator@yahoo.com",
                        "username": "geissonator"
                    },
                    "message": "I'm not totally sold on the motivation here. The only people who could manually start/stop the service \"should\" know what they're doing? i.e. They've got root ssh access into the BMC. Do you have situations where other non-technical people can get into a system and run this? And ... who would even know to run this command to stop it? The whole thing just seems a bit overboard to me."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 18,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Eric Yang",
                        "email": "eric.yang.wiwynn@gmail.com",
                        "username": "fornchu110"
                    },
                    "message": "The object mapper can indirectly affect many BMC services, and when it fails to initialize properly, the resulting impact can vary in form, making it difficult to trace the root cause.\n\nDuring the production and development process, many people are involved\u2014including those working on automation validation and tool development. While they may know how to use commands like systemctl stop or restart, they may not fully understand the potential consequences of stopping or restarting the object mapper. Given the number of people and tools involved, it\u2019s also difficult to quickly trace which commands were run or which tools were used.\n\nThat\u2019s why I proposed adding an option to prevent manual stop/restart of the object mapper. From my personal experience, after applying this restriction, I haven\u2019t seen any object mapper-related issues in the journal caused by unintended restarts."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 18,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Eric Yang",
                        "email": "eric.yang.wiwynn@gmail.com",
                        "username": "fornchu110"
                    },
                    "message": "Done"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Brad Bishop",
                        "email": "bradleyb@fuzziesquirrel.com",
                        "username": "bradbishop"
                    },
                    "message": "In yocto terms a change such as this is called distro policy.  Upstream we only support one set of distro policy and you can think of that set as as a collection of defaults that make sense for most OpenBMC integrators.  I don't think this change meets that criteria.\n\nYou are of course free to create your own layer, add it to your build configuration, and apply whatever distro policy you want in that layer."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Eric Yang",
                        "email": "eric.yang.wiwynn@gmail.com",
                        "username": "fornchu110"
                    },
                    "message": "Understood. I\u2019ll proceed to test it through the platform configuration. Thank you all again for your guidance!"
                }
            ],
